package pages

// The content of the new-character dialog: one question, asked once.
// Creation used to be a page carrying the whole sheet behind a Save button --
// the last place in the app that held unsaved work. It is now a name and a
// redirect to the editor, which saves each panel as it is edited. So this
// fragment has one field, and the thing it creates is a row the editor can
// fill in rather than a finished character.
//
// NewCharacterPanel names the error block. It is a constant because it appears
// three times -- the block's own id, this form's two targeting attributes -- and
// once more in the handler that renders the block on a rejection. A typo in any
// one of them fails silently: the reply would land nowhere and the dialog would
// sit there looking like nothing had happened.
const NewCharacterPanel = "new-character"

// The 422 needs a route of its own. base.templ configures noSwap for the whole
// 4xx range, so a rejected name would otherwise replace nothing at all. htmx
// tests the exact code against those entries before it looks for an hx-status
// override, which is why "422" misses the "4xx" entry and finds this. The
// editor's panels carry the same attribute for the same reason.
//
// Both replies land on the error block rather than on the form, and that is
// deliberate: replacing the form would discard the name the user just typed
// unless the handler echoed it back, and there is no reason to make the handler
// responsible for redrawing a field it did not change.
//
// A successful create answers with HX-Redirect and no body, so nothing lands
// here at all -- the navigation takes the dialog with it.
//
// `required` is safe on this field even though form-validity.js suppresses the
// browser's own bubble. htmx validates a form before posting it and abandons
// the request when the form is invalid, which on a single deliberate press
// could have meant a button that does nothing and says nothing -- but textField
// carries DaisyUI's .validator, so an empty name explains itself underneath the
// field with no request and no script.
//
// Close dispatches modal:close rather than submitting a <form method="dialog">,
// which is what every other dialog in the app closes with. It cannot use one
// here: this button sits inside the form that posts the name, and a form does
// not nest. The event is the same one the shell already listens for, so the
// dialog closes the same way whether a person presses this or a handler asks
// for it with an HX-Trigger.
//
// There is no placeholder on the field. A placeholder is for a format that
// needs demonstrating -- "Cleric 3, Fighter 1" earns one, a name does not, and
// an example name in a box asking for a name reads as a value already filled in.
//
// There is no maxlength here. It would cap typing without ever explaining why,
// and the column's 128 characters are enforced in the handler, which can say so
// in words. Adding the attribute would also mean a new parameter on textField
// and a change at every one of its call sites, for a constraint that is already
// checked where it matters.
templ NewCharacterFragment() {
	{{ errorBlock := "#errors-" + NewCharacterPanel }}
	<div class="grid gap-4">
		<h3 class="m-0 pr-10 font-serif text-xl leading-none">New Character</h3>
		<form
			class="grid gap-4"
			hx-post="/characters"
			hx-target={ errorBlock }
			hx-swap="outerHTML"
			hx-status:422={ "target:" + errorBlock + ",swap:outerHTML" }
		>
			@PanelFormErrors(NewCharacterPanel, []string{})
			@textField("name", "Character Name", "", "", true)
			<div class="modal-action">
				<button
					type="button"
					class="btn border-base-content/50"
					hx-on:click="window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('modal:close'))"
				>Close</button>
				<button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Create character</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</div>
}
